### Proxy API Requests in next.config.ts

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/docs/01-app/02-guides/migrating/from-create-react-app.mdx

Use the rewrites configuration to forward API requests to a backend server, replacing the CRA proxy field.

```ts
import { NextConfig } from 'next'

const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  async rewrites() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/api/:path*',
        destination: 'https://your-backend.com/:path*',
      },
    ]
  },
}
```

--------------------------------

### Configure static export in next.config.js

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/docs/01-app/02-guides/static-exports.mdx

Set the output mode to 'export' in your Next.js configuration file to enable static site generation.

```javascript
/**
 * @type {import('next').NextConfig}
 */
const nextConfig = {
  output: 'export',

  // Optional: Change links `/me` -> `/me/` and emit `/me.html` -> `/me/index.html`
  // trailingSlash: true,

  // Optional: Prevent automatic `/me` -> `/me/`, instead preserve `href`
  // skipTrailingSlashRedirect: true,

  // Optional: Change the output directory `out` -> `dist`
  // distDir: 'dist',
}

module.exports = nextConfig
```

--------------------------------

### Warning when rewrites are used with output: export

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/packages/next/src/server/config.ts

During config validation in next build, if rewrites, redirects, or headers are configured alongside output: 'export', Next.js emits a warning that these custom routes will not work in static export mode. The check is gated behind `!hasNextSupport` to allow internal Next.js usage.

```typescript
  if (result.output === 'export') {
    if (result.i18n) {
      throw new Error(
        'Specified "i18n" cannot be used with "output: export". See more info here: https://nextjs.org/docs/messages/export-no-i18n'
      )
    }

    if (!hasNextSupport) {
      if (result.rewrites) {
        Log.warn(
          'Specified "rewrites" will not automatically work with "output: export". See more info here: https://nextjs.org/docs/messages/export-no-custom-routes'
        )
      }
      if (result.redirects) {
        Log.warn(
          'Specified "redirects" will not automatically work with "output: export". See more info here: https://nextjs.org/docs/messages/export-no-custom-routes'
        )
      }
      if (result.headers) {
        Log.warn(
          'Specified "headers" will not automatically work with "output: export". See more info here: https://nextjs.org/docs/messages/export-no-custom-routes'
        )
      }
    }
  }
```

### Self-Hosting > Proxy

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/docs/01-app/02-guides/self-hosting.mdx

Proxy works self-hosted with zero configuration when deploying with next start, but is not supported when using static exports because it requires access to incoming requests. Logic requiring Node.js APIs can often be placed in Server Component layouts (such as inspecting headers and redirecting), managed through redirects and rewrites in next.config.js, or implemented using a custom server.

--------------------------------

### Additional Considerations > Proxying API Requests

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/docs/01-app/02-guides/migrating/from-create-react-app.mdx

If your previous application relied on a proxy field in package.json to forward requests to a backend server, you can replicate this functionality in Next.js by using the rewrites configuration in next.config.ts.

