### Configure AWS S3 Object Resource

Source: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-aws/blob/v6.33.0/website/docs/cdktf/python/r/s3_object.html.markdown

This section details the arguments available for defining and managing an S3 object using the `aws_s3_object` Terraform resource. It covers required parameters like bucket and key, as well as optional settings for ACLs, encryption, caching, and metadata.

```APIDOC
## RESOURCE aws_s3_object

### Description
Manages an S3 object within an AWS S3 bucket. This resource allows you to upload files, set metadata, configure access control, and manage encryption settings for objects stored in S3.

### Method
CREATE/UPDATE

### Resource Name
aws_s3_object

### Arguments
#### Required Arguments
- **bucket** (string) - Required - Name of the bucket to put the file in. Alternatively, an [S3 access point](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/using-access-points.html) ARN can be specified.
- **key** (string) - Required - Name of the object once it is in the bucket.

#### Optional Arguments
- **acl** (string) - Optional - [Canned ACL](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/acl-overview.html#canned-acl) to apply. Valid values are `private`, `public-read`, `public-read-write`, `aws-exec-read`, `authenticated-read`, `bucket-owner-read`, and `bucket-owner-full-control`.
- **bucket_key_enabled** (boolean) - Optional - Whether or not to use [Amazon S3 Bucket Keys](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/bucket-key.html) for SSE-KMS.
- **cache_control** (string) - Optional - Caching behavior along the request/reply chain. Read [w3c cache_control](http://www.w3.org/Protocols/rfc2616/rfc2616-sec14.html#sec14.9) for further details.
- **checksum_algorithm** (string) - Optional - Indicates the algorithm used to create the checksum for the object. If a value is specified and the object is encrypted with KMS, you must have permission to use the `kms:Decrypt` action. Valid values: `CRC32`, `CRC32C`, `CRC64NVME`, `SHA1`, `SHA256`.
- **content_base64** (string) - Optional, conflicts with `source` and `content` - Base64-encoded data that will be decoded and uploaded as raw bytes for the object content. This allows safely uploading non-UTF8 binary data, but is recommended only for small content such as the result of the `gzipbase64` function with small text strings. For larger objects, use `source` to stream the content from a disk file.
- **content_disposition** (string) - Optional - Presentational information for the object. Read [w3c content_disposition](http://www.w3.org/Protocols/rfc2616/rfc2616-sec19.1.html#sec19.5.1) for further information.
- **content_encoding** (string) - Optional - Content encodings that have been applied to the object and thus what decoding mechanisms must be applied to obtain the media-type referenced by the Content-Type header field. Read [w3c content encoding](http://www.w3.org/Protocols/rfc2616/rfc2616-sec14.1.html#sec14.11) for further information.
- **content_language** (string) - Optional - Language the content is in e.g., en-US or en-GB.
- **content_type** (string) - Optional - Standard MIME type describing the format of the object data, e.g., application/octet-stream. All Valid MIME Types are valid for this input.
- **content** (string) - Optional, conflicts with `source` and `content_base64` - Literal string value to use as the object content, which will be uploaded as UTF-8-encoded text.
- **etag** (string) - Optional - Triggers updates when the value changes. The only meaningful value is `filemd5("path/to/file")` (Terraform 0.11.12 or later) or `${md5(file("path/to/file"))}` (Terraform 0.11.11 or earlier). This attribute is not compatible with KMS encryption, `kms_key_id` or `server_side_encryption = "aws:kms"`, also if an object is larger than 16 MB, the AWS Management Console will upload or copy that object as a Multipart Upload, and therefore the ETag will not be an MD5 digest (see `source_hash` instead).
- **force_destroy** (boolean) - Optional - Whether to allow the object to be deleted by removing any legal hold on any object version. Default is `false`. This value should be set to `true` only if the bucket has S3 object lock enabled.
- **kms_key_id** (string) - Optional - ARN of the KMS Key to use for object encryption. If the S3 Bucket has server-side encryption enabled, that value will automatically be used. If referencing the `aws_kms_key` resource, use the `arn` attribute. If referencing the `aws_kms_alias` data source or resource, use the `target_key_arn` attribute. Terraform will only perform drift detection if a configuration value is provided.
- **metadata** (map) - Optional - Map of keys/values to provision metadata (will be automatically prefixed by `x-amz-meta-`, note that only lowercase label are currently supported by the AWS Go API).
- **object_lock_legal_hold_status** (string) - Optional - [Legal hold](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lock-overview.html#object-lock-legal-holds) status that you want to apply to the specified object. Valid values are `ON` and `OFF`.
- **object_lock_mode** (string) - Optional - Object lock [retention mode](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lock-overview.html#object-lock-retention-modes) that you want to apply to this object. Valid values are `GOVERNANCE` and `COMPLIANCE`.
- **object_lock_retain_until_date** (string) - Optional - Date and time, in [RFC3339 format](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3339#section-5.8), when this object's object lock will [expire](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lock-overview.html#object-lock-retention-periods).
- **override_provider** (object) - Optional - Override provider-level configuration options. See [Override Provider](#override-provider) below for more details.

### Request Example
```hcl
resource "aws_s3_object" "example" {
  bucket = "my-example-bucket"
  key    = "my-object-key.txt"
  content = "Hello, S3!"
  acl    = "private"
  tags = {
    Environment = "Dev"
  }
}
```

### Attributes Reference
#### Exported Attributes
- **arn** (string) - The ARN of the object.
- **version_id** (string) - A unique version ID for the object.
- **etag** (string) - The ETag of the object.
- **id** (string) - The `key` of the object.

#### Example Attributes
```json
{
  "arn": "arn:aws:s3:::my-example-bucket/my-object-key.txt",
  "version_id": null,
  "etag": "d41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e",
  "id": "my-object-key.txt"
}
```
```

--------------------------------

### Set source hash for S3 object updates

Source: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-aws/blob/v6.33.0/website/docs/r/s3_object.html.markdown

Configures the source_hash attribute using the filemd5 function to track file changes. This ensures Terraform detects updates even when ETag encryption limitations are present in Terraform 0.11.12 or later.

```terraform
source_hash = filemd5("path/to/source")
```

--------------------------------

### S3 Object Hash Calculation Functions

Source: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-aws/blob/v6.33.0/website/docs/cdktf/typescript/r/s3_bucket_object.html.markdown

Terraform functions used to calculate the MD5 hash of a local file for the etag and sourceHash arguments to trigger resource updates when file content changes.

```hcl
filemd5("path/to/file")
${md5(file("path/to/file"))}
filemd5("path/to/source")
```

--------------------------------

### UpdateDistribution is called for every resource with detected changes

Source: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-aws/blob/main/internal/service/cloudfront/distribution.go

When a plan shows any origin block diff (including spurious response_completion_timeout changes), terraform apply calls UpdateDistribution for THAT resource. There is no mechanism to skip resources that only have attribute-level noise

```go
func resourceDistributionUpdate(ctx context.Context, d *schema.ResourceData, meta any) diag.Diagnostics {
	var diags diag.Diagnostics
	conn := meta.(*conns.AWSClient).CloudFrontClient(ctx)

	if d.HasChangesExcept(names.AttrTags, names.AttrTagsAll) {
		input := cloudfront.UpdateDistributionInput{
			DistributionConfig: expandDistributionConfig(d),
			Id:                 aws.String(d.Id()),
			IfMatch:            aws.String(d.Get("etag").(string)),
		}

		const (
			timeout = 1 * time.Minute
		)
		_, err := tfresource.RetryWhenIsA[any, *awstypes.InvalidViewerCertificate](ctx, timeout, func(ctx context.Context) (any, error) {
			return conn.UpdateDistribution(ctx, &input)
		})

		if errs.IsA[*awstypes.PreconditionFailed](err) {
			var etag string
			etag, err = distroETag(ctx, conn, d.Id())

			if err != nil {
				return sdkdiag.AppendFromErr(diags, err)
			}

			input.IfMatch = aws.String(etag)

			_, err = conn.UpdateDistribution(ctx, &input)
		}

		if err != nil {
			return sdkdiag.AppendErrorf(diags, "updating CloudFront Distribution (%s): %s", d.Id(), err)
		}

		if d.Get("wait_for_deployment").(bool) {
			if _, err := waitDistributionDeployed(ctx, conn, d.Id()); err != nil {
				return sdkdiag.AppendErrorf(diags, "waiting for CloudFront Distribution (%s) deploy: %s", d.Id(), err)
			}
		}
	}

	return append(diags, resourceDistributionRead(ctx, d, meta)...)
}
```

--------------------------------

### Update function — no invalidation is created when certificate changes

Source: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-aws/blob/main/internal/service/cloudfront/distribution.go

This is the complete update handler for aws_cloudfront_distribution. When any attribute changes (including acm_certificate_arn or iam_certificate_id), it calls UpdateDistribution with retry on InvalidViewerCertificate for eventual consistency, then waits for deployment. There is no CreateInvalidation call, confirming no automatic invalidation is triggered on certificate change.

```go
func resourceDistributionUpdate(ctx context.Context, d *schema.ResourceData, meta any) diag.Diagnostics {
	var diags diag.Diagnostics
	conn := meta.(*conns.AWSClient).CloudFrontClient(ctx)

	if d.HasChangesExcept(names.AttrTags, names.AttrTagsAll) {
		input := cloudfront.UpdateDistributionInput{
			DistributionConfig: expandDistributionConfig(d),
			Id:                 aws.String(d.Id()),
			IfMatch:            aws.String(d.Get("etag").(string)),
		}

		// ACM and IAM certificate eventual consistency.
		// InvalidViewerCertificate: The specified SSL certificate doesn't exist, isn't in us-east-1 region, isn't valid, or doesn't include a valid certificate chain.
		const (
			timeout = 1 * time.Minute
		)
		_, err := tfresource.RetryWhenIsA[any, *awstypes.InvalidViewerCertificate](ctx, timeout, func(ctx context.Context) (any, error) {
			return conn.UpdateDistribution(ctx, &input)
		})

		// Refresh our ETag if it is out of date and attempt update again.
		if errs.IsA[*awstypes.PreconditionFailed](err) {
			var etag string
			etag, err = distroETag(ctx, conn, d.Id())

			if err != nil {
				return sdkdiag.AppendFromErr(diags, err)
			}

			input.IfMatch = aws.String(etag)

			_, err = conn.UpdateDistribution(ctx, &input)
		}

		if err != nil {
			return sdkdiag.AppendErrorf(diags, "updating CloudFront Distribution (%s): %s", d.Id(), err)
		}

		if d.Get("wait_for_deployment").(bool) {
			if _, err := waitDistributionDeployed(ctx, conn, d.Id()); err != nil {
				return sdkdiag.AppendErrorf(diags, "waiting for CloudFront Distribution (%s) deploy: %s", d.Id(), err)
			}
		}
	}

	return append(diags, resourceDistributionRead(ctx, d, meta)...)
}
```

### Invalidate Specific CloudFront Paths with Terraform

Source: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-aws/blob/v6.33.0/website/docs/actions/cloudfront_create_invalidation.html.markdown

Shows how to invalidate a list of specific file paths and directory patterns (e.g., `/images/*`, `/css/*`, `/js/app.js`) within a CloudFront distribution, including setting a custom timeout for the invalidation request.

```terraform
action "aws_cloudfront_create_invalidation" "assets" {
  config {
    distribution_id = aws_cloudfront_distribution.example.id
    paths = [
      "/images/*",
      "/css/*",
      "/js/app.js",
      "/index.html"
    ]
    timeout = 1200 # 20 minutes
  }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Create a Basic CloudFront Invalidation with Terraform

Source: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-aws/blob/v6.33.0/website/docs/actions/cloudfront_create_invalidation.html.markdown

Demonstrates how to create a basic CloudFront cache invalidation for an entire distribution (`/*`) using the `aws_cloudfront_create_invalidation` action. It integrates with a `terraform_data` resource to trigger the invalidation on resource creation or update.

```terraform
resource "aws_cloudfront_distribution" "example" {
  # ... distribution configuration
}

action "aws_cloudfront_create_invalidation" "example" {
  config {
    distribution_id = aws_cloudfront_distribution.example.id
    paths           = ["/*"]
  }
}

resource "terraform_data" "example" {
  input = "trigger-invalidation"

  lifecycle {
    action_trigger {
      events  = [before_create, before_update]
      actions = [action.aws_cloudfront_create_invalidation.example]
    }
  }
}
```

--------------------------------

### CloudFront Invalidation with Custom Caller Reference in Terraform

Source: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-aws/blob/v6.33.0/website/docs/actions/cloudfront_create_invalidation.html.markdown

Illustrates how to provide a unique `caller_reference` for a CloudFront invalidation request, useful for tracking and idempotency. It also demonstrates setting a custom timeout.

```terraform
action "aws_cloudfront_create_invalidation" "deployment" {
  config {
    distribution_id  = aws_cloudfront_distribution.example.id
    paths            = ["/*"]
    caller_reference = "deployment-${formatdate("YYYY-MM-DD-hhmm", timestamp())}"
    timeout          = 900
  }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Integrate CloudFront Invalidation into CI/CD Pipeline with Terraform

Source: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-aws/blob/v6.33.0/website/docs/actions/cloudfront_create_invalidation.html.markdown

Provides an example of integrating CloudFront cache invalidation into a CI/CD pipeline, triggering it after static asset deployment (e.g., S3 sync). It invalidates specific paths relevant to a deployment.

```terraform
# Trigger invalidation after S3 sync
resource "terraform_data" "deploy_complete" {
  input = local.deployment_id

  lifecycle {
    action_trigger {
      events  = [before_create, before_update]
      actions = [action.aws_cloudfront_create_invalidation.post_deploy]
    }
  }

  depends_on = [aws_s3_object.assets]
}

action "aws_cloudfront_create_invalidation" "post_deploy" {
  config {
    distribution_id = aws_cloudfront_distribution.main.id
    paths = [
      "/index.html",
      "/manifest.json",
      "/static/js/*",
      "/static/css/*"
    ]
  }
}
```

### Action: aws_cloudfront_create_invalidation

Source: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-aws/blob/v6.33.0/website/docs/cdktf/python/actions/cloudfront_create_invalidation.html.markdown

`aws_cloudfront_create_invalidation` is currently in beta. Its interface and behavior may change as the feature evolves, and breaking changes are possible. It is offered as a technical preview without compatibility guarantees until Terraform 1.14 is generally available. This action invalidates CloudFront distribution cache for specified paths by creating an invalidation request and waiting for it to complete.

